 "drivingQuestion": "From where is it derived that those liable provisional guilt offerings are exempt after Yom Kippur?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "R' Elazar supplies the verse",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "keritot.25b.18",
   "daf": "25b",
   "sentenceId": 17,
   "he": "חַיָּיבֵי אֲשָׁמוֹת תְּלוּיִין כּוּ׳. מְנָהָנֵי מִילֵּי? אָמַר רַבִּי אֶלְעָזָר, אֲמַר קְרָא: ״מִכֹּל חַטֹּאתֵיכֶם לִפְנֵי ה׳ וְגוֹ׳״ – חֵטְא שֶׁאֵין מַכִּיר בּוֹ אֶלָּא הַמָּקוֹם – יוֹם הַכִּפּוּרִים מְכַפֵּר.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: Those «liable» to bring «provisional guilt offerings» are exempt from bringing them after Yom Kippur. The Gemara asks: «From where is this matter» derived? «Rabbi Elazar said:» With regard to Yom Kippur «the verse states:» “For on this day shall atonement be made for you, to cleanse you; «from all your sins» you shall be pure «before the Lord”» (Leviticus 16:30). This indicates that «Yom Kippur atones» for «a sin of which only the Omnipresent is aware,» i.e., an uncertain sin that renders one liable to bring a provisional guilt offering."
  }
 ],
